+ '<h2 class="l-title">2. Row Selection \u2014 .loc vs .iloc</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text">Pandas provides two primary indexers for row selection: <code>.loc</code> for <strong>label-based</strong> access and <code>.iloc</code> for <strong>integer position-based</strong> access. Understanding the difference is critical \u2014 mixing them up is one of the most common Pandas mistakes.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare">'
+ '<div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">.loc (Label-based)</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Uses <strong>index labels</strong> (strings, dates, etc.)</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">End of slice is <strong>inclusive</strong></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">df.loc["a":"c"] includes "c"</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Best for: named indices, date ranges</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Raises KeyError on missing labels</div></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">.iloc (Integer Position)</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Uses <strong>integer positions</strong> (0, 1, 2...)</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">End of slice is <strong>exclusive</strong></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">df.iloc[0:3] excludes position 3</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Best for: first N rows, last N rows</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Raises IndexError on out-of-range</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"name"</span>: [<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="str">"Carol"</span>, <span class="str">"Dave"</span>, <span class="str">"Eve"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"age"</span>: [<span class="num">25</span>, <span class="num">30</span>, <span class="num">28</span>, <span class="num">35</span>, <span class="num">22</span>],\n    <span class="str">"score"</span>: [<span class="num">88</span>, <span class="num">72</span>, <span class="num">95</span>, <span class="num">60</span>, <span class="num">91</span>]\n}, index=[<span class="str">"s1"</span>, <span class="str">"s2"</span>, <span class="str">"s3"</span>, <span class="str">"s4"</span>, <span class="str">"s5"</span>])\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- .loc: label-based ----</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">loc</span>[<span class="str">"s1"</span>]                <span class="cm"># single row as Series</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">loc</span>[[<span class="str">"s1"</span>, <span class="str">"s3"</span>]]         <span class="cm"># multiple rows as DataFrame</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">loc</span>[<span class="str">"s1"</span>:<span class="str">"s3"</span>]            <span class="cm"># slice: s1, s2, s3 (inclusive!)</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">loc</span>[<span class="str">"s2"</span>, <span class="str">"name"</span>]        <span class="cm"># single value: \'Bob\'</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">loc</span>[<span class="str">"s1"</span>:<span class="str">"s3"</span>, [<span class="str">"name"</span>, <span class="str">"score"</span>]]  <span class="cm"># rows + columns</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- .iloc: integer position ----</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">iloc</span>[<span class="num">0</span>]                   <span class="cm"># first row as Series</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">iloc</span>[[<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">2</span>]]              <span class="cm"># rows at positions 0 and 2</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">iloc</span>[<span class="num">0</span>:<span class="num">3</span>]                 <span class="cm"># first 3 rows (exclusive end!)</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">iloc</span>[<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">0</span>]               <span class="cm"># row 1, col 0: \'Bob\'</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">iloc</span>[<span class="num">0</span>:<span class="num">3</span>, [<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">2</span>]]        <span class="cm"># first 3 rows, columns 0 and 2</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- .at / .iat: fast scalar access ----</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">at</span>[<span class="str">"s1"</span>, <span class="str">"name"</span>]         <span class="cm"># \'Alice\' (label-based, fast)</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">iat</span>[<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">0</span>]                 <span class="cm"># \'Alice\' (integer-based, fast)</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># .at/.iat are ~10x faster than .loc/.iloc for single values</span>\n<span class="cm"># Use them inside loops when you must access individual cells</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps">'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Need a label or name?</div><div class="step-detail">Use <code>.loc</code> for label-based selection. Works with string indices, date indices, and boolean masks. Slice endpoints are <strong>inclusive</strong>.</div></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Need a position?</div><div class="step-detail">Use <code>.iloc</code> for integer position-based selection. Works like Python list indexing. Slice endpoints are <strong>exclusive</strong>.</div></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Need a single value fast?</div><div class="step-detail">Use <code>.at</code> (label) or <code>.iat</code> (integer) for scalar access. ~10x faster than loc/iloc for individual cells. Essential inside loops.</div></div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><strong>Think about it:</strong> Why does <code>.loc["a":"c"]</code> include "c" but <code>.iloc[0:3]</code> excludes position 3? Label-based slicing is inclusive because labels are not ordered integers \u2014 there is no "next" label after "c" to use as the exclusive endpoint. This matches how you think about ranges of names or dates.</div>'

