+ '<h2 class="l-title">3. Boolean Filtering</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Boolean indexing</strong> is the most powerful way to filter rows. You create a boolean Series (True/False for each row) and use it to select only the rows where the condition is True. This is analogous to SQL\'s WHERE clause.</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"name"</span>: [<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="str">"Carol"</span>, <span class="str">"Dave"</span>, <span class="str">"Eve"</span>, <span class="str">"Frank"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"age"</span>: [<span class="num">25</span>, <span class="num">30</span>, <span class="num">28</span>, <span class="num">35</span>, <span class="num">22</span>, <span class="num">40</span>],\n    <span class="str">"score"</span>: [<span class="num">88</span>, <span class="num">72</span>, <span class="num">95</span>, <span class="num">60</span>, <span class="num">91</span>, <span class="num">85</span>],\n    <span class="str">"field"</span>: [<span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>, <span class="str">"RL"</span>, <span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>, <span class="str">"NLP"</span>]\n})\n\n<span class="cm"># Single condition</span>\nhigh_scorers = df[df[<span class="str">"score"</span>] > <span class="num">85</span>]\n<span class="fn">print</span>(high_scorers)\n<span class="cm">#     name  age  score field</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0  Alice   25     88   NLP</span>\n<span class="cm"># 2  Carol   28     95    RL</span>\n<span class="cm"># 4    Eve   22     91    CV</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Multiple conditions: MUST use & | ~ and parentheses!</span>\nresult = df[(df[<span class="str">"score"</span>] > <span class="num">80</span>) & (df[<span class="str">"age"</span>] < <span class="num">30</span>)]\n<span class="fn">print</span>(result)\n<span class="cm">#     name  age  score field</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0  Alice   25     88   NLP</span>\n<span class="cm"># 4    Eve   22     91    CV</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># OR condition</span>\nnlp_or_cv = df[(df[<span class="str">"field"</span>] == <span class="str">"NLP"</span>) | (df[<span class="str">"field"</span>] == <span class="str">"CV"</span>)]\n\n<span class="cm"># NOT condition</span>\nnot_nlp = df[~(df[<span class="str">"field"</span>] == <span class="str">"NLP"</span>)]\n\n<span class="cm"># .isin() \u2014 cleaner than chained OR</span>\nnlp_cv = df[df[<span class="str">"field"</span>].<span class="fn">isin</span>([<span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>])]\n\n<span class="cm"># .between() \u2014 range filter (inclusive by default)</span>\nmid_age = df[df[<span class="str">"age"</span>].<span class="fn">between</span>(<span class="num">25</span>, <span class="num">35</span>)]\n<span class="fn">print</span>(mid_age[<span class="str">"name"</span>].tolist())  <span class="cm"># [\'Alice\', \'Bob\', \'Carol\', \'Dave\']</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Common mistake:</strong> Using <code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, <code>not</code> instead of <code>&amp;</code>, <code>|</code>, <code>~</code> will raise a ValueError. Python\'s <code>and</code>/<code>or</code> work on single booleans, but Pandas boolean Series need element-wise operators. Also, <strong>always wrap each condition in parentheses</strong> due to operator precedence.</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># .query() \u2014 SQL-like filtering syntax</span>\n<span class="cm"># More readable for complex conditions</span>\n\nresult = df.<span class="fn">query</span>(<span class="str">"score > 80 and age < 30"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(result)\n\n<span class="cm"># Variables with @</span>\nmin_score = <span class="num">85</span>\nresult = df.<span class="fn">query</span>(<span class="str">"score >= @min_score"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># String comparison in query</span>\nresult = df.<span class="fn">query</span>(<span class="str">"field == \'NLP\'"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Negation</span>\nresult = df.<span class="fn">query</span>(<span class="str">"field != \'NLP\' and score > 70"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># .query() is also faster on large DataFrames because</span>\n<span class="cm"># it avoids creating intermediate boolean arrays</span></code></pre></div>'

